In article <13719@phoenix.Princeton.EDU> kerog@phoenix.Princeton.EDU (Keith Eric Rogers) writes:
>I have been playing the Mac version of 2.3 and I seem to have discovered
>something which my friend claims has never been mentioned on this
>newsgroup.  
>When you are polymorphed into a human and are asked what your new name
>is, it seems that your choice can have quite interesting effects.  The
>first time it happened, I tried Roland, and got 18/** strength.  This
>has not proved to be consistent, but certain other names have gotten me
>strengths on the same order.  I tried Caesar, Alexander, Gilgamesh,
>Goliath, Gandalf, and Superman, among others.  Most of these gave me
>18/** strength, except for Gandalf.  Superman seems to work more
>consistently than the others.  ...

For a while, this was the bug that wouldn't die.  Originally polymorph-self
had no strength change at all.  Somehow, somewhere, someone stuck in a change
to give you high strength when you turn into a strong monster.  You got strong
when you turned into the monster, and then got your old strength back upon
becoming human.

Unfortunately it didn't take into account anything other than polymorph
from human to monster, and polymorph ending when you go from monster to
human.  It specifically didn't cound monster to monster, or polymorphing
directly into a "new (wo)man".  So you can get high strength which carries
over from becoming a monster.

18/98 comes up when you had 18/** and then your strength went down.

Needless to say, 3.0 fixes all of that.

Also, the name you choose has absolutely no effect, nor does the gender.
This certainly shows the human tendency to see patterns where none exist...
